onomic  policy for northern ireland compared to  the rest of the uk i think that's the  thing that  will in that could potentially intensify  violence and  i i make this prediction i think the  next fears  of loyalist response after the scenes  you're seeing now  the street disorder will be some attack  in the next few months on the  institutions of the protocol  in ports for example physical  infrastructure  which may come under attack i think my  sources are certainly telling me that  in belfast within the ulster loyalist  community that would be a worrying  escalation henry we've seen northern  ireland leaders  condemning the arrests saying it's  completely unacceptable what can they do  to stop this  well pardon calling for it and having a  security response  at the end of the day it will be called  off or intensified by the loyalist part  of militaries  okay now if they're very listened to by  unionist politicians  it you will see a de-escalation but i  think everything hinges  on what happens to the protocol will  the british government make it less  punishing on the economy in northern  ireland well  there be less checks at the ports  for for goods coming into northern  ireland than this and the  lesson the impression that this  uh this part of the uk is no longer  it's being cut off because it's in the  eu single market  right the problem is the argument over  brexit and  the eu is poisoning the atmosphere has  been for some time i've been warning  about this coming  and now we see it manifested on the  streets  but i think we could be in for an  extremely turbulent summer  and even autumn and left the thing is uh  and this thing is addressed properly  and that requires brussels and dublin as  well as london  we'll have to leave it there author and  journalist henry mcdonald speaking to us  from belfast  thank you very much
 

